    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>Joint pain around menopause is common, affecting roughly seven in ten women in the transition, and falling oestrogen is the most likely common thread.</strong> In the largest randomised trial, oestrogen alone cut the share of women reporting joint pain only modestly, from 79.2% to 76.3% after one year.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>How common:</strong> a 2024 review in Climacteric put musculoskeletal pain in perimenopausal women at about 71%, and named it the musculoskeletal syndrome of menopause.</li>
        <li><strong>Hormone therapy:</strong> a real but small effect in the WHI oestrogen-alone trial of 10,739 women, with no change in joint swelling. It is a decision for your gynaecologist, not a painkiller.</li>
        <li><strong>What comes first:</strong> strength training, weight management and ruling out other causes such as thyroid disease, rheumatoid arthritis and vitamin D deficiency.</li>
        <li><strong>The honest limitation:</strong> no published trial has tested collagen, glucosamine or any other supplement specifically for menopausal joint pain. The trials were run in osteoarthritis.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

