        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        The mechanism is plausible rather than proven. Oestrogen receptors sit on cartilage,
        synovium, tendon and bone, and oestrogen influences inflammation and pain processing. When it
        falls, several tissues lose a regulator at the same time, which is why the complaint is often
        "everything aches" rather than one joint. In clinic the most common version is morning
        stiffness in the hands and feet, an ache in the knees on stairs, and a shoulder that suddenly
        will not reach the top shelf.
      </p>

      <h2 id="hrt">Does HRT help menopause joint pain?</h2>

      <p>
        Modestly. The best randomised evidence is a secondary analysis of the Women's Health
        Initiative oestrogen-alone trial, published in <em>Menopause</em> in 2013, and it showed a
        real but small effect.
      </p>

      <p>
        Among 10,739 women who had had a hysterectomy, 77% reported joint pain at the start. After one
        year, joint pain was reported by 76.3% of women on oestrogen against 79.2% on placebo. In a
        subset followed for three years the gap was 74.2% against 79.8%. Joint swelling did not
        change. The lead author called the benefit modest but sustained, and stressed that it has to
        be weighed against the known risks of hormone therapy.
      </p>

      <div class="jr-table-scroll">
        <table class="jr-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">What was measured</th><th scope="col">Oestrogen</th><th scope="col">Placebo</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Joint pain after 1 year</th><td>76.3%</td><td>79.2%</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Joint pain after 3 years (subset)</th><td>74.2%</td><td>79.8%</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Joint swelling</th><td>No difference</td><td>No difference</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        Two limits matter. These were women without a uterus taking oestrogen alone, so the result
        does not transfer neatly to women who need a progestogen as well. And a three to five
        percentage point reduction is not the relief most women are hoping for. If you are
        considering hormone therapy for hot flushes, sleep or bone protection, the joint effect is a
        reasonable small bonus to discuss with your gynaecologist. It is not, on this evidence, a
        reason to start it for joints alone.
      </p>

      <h2 id="what-helps">What actually helps menopause joint pain?</h2>

      <p>
        Loading the joints and the muscles around them, before anything you can swallow. This is the
        same order of priorities I would give any patient over 45 with aching knees, and menopause
        makes it more urgent because muscle and bone are being lost at the same time.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Strength training, two to three sessions a week.</strong> Muscle that supports a joint takes load off the cartilage, and resistance work is also the most effective thing a woman can do for <a href="/pages/how-to-increase-bone-density">bone density after menopause</a>. A pilot trial in Singapore, the MUSE study, is now testing muscle strengthening against oestrogen and the two combined for menopausal joint pain, which tells you how seriously clinicians take it.</li>
        <li><strong>Weight, if it has crept up.</strong> Every kilogram matters to a knee. The American College of Rheumatology's 2026 osteoarthritis update added a conditional recommendation for GLP-1 medicines in knee osteoarthritis with obesity, a sign of how much load drives pain.</li>
        <li><strong>Sleep and activity pacing.</strong> Poor sleep lowers pain thresholds, and menopause disrupts sleep. Treating night sweats often helps the aches indirectly.</li>
        <li><strong>A blood test before a bottle.</strong> Vitamin D, thyroid function, and if the small joints of the hands are swollen, markers for inflammatory arthritis. Several of these are treatable causes that look exactly like menopausal aching. Our guide to <a href="/pages/vitamin-d-deficiency-symptoms">vitamin D deficiency symptoms</a> explains which signs are specific.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="supplements">Do supplements help menopause joint pain?</h2>

      <p>
        Nobody knows, because almost nobody has tested them in this group. The supplement trials for
        joint pain were run in people with diagnosed knee osteoarthritis, in athletes, or in mixed
        older adults. Menopausal joint pain has not been studied as its own outcome.
      </p>

      <p>
        The nearest evidence is collagen in knee osteoarthritis, which we cover in detail in
        <a href="/pages/collagen-for-joint-pain">collagen for joint pain</a>. A 2024 trial sequential
        meta-analysis of 35 randomised trials found a moderate improvement in pain scores, and
        several of those trials enrolled mostly women over 50. That is suggestive, not proof for
        menopause specifically. A registered trial is now testing 12g of collagen peptides daily for
        12 months in 302 postmenopausal women with knee osteoarthritis. When it reports, it will be
        the first direct answer.
      </p>

