      <p>
        The ingredients on our own label deserve the same honesty. Skelecoll's 10,000mg of
        hydrolysed collagen sits within the range used in the osteoarthritis trials. Its 300mg of
        glucosamine HCl is a fifth of the 1,500mg studied, and the American College of Rheumatology's
        September 2026 guideline recommends against glucosamine for osteoarthritis altogether, so
        that ingredient is not a reason to buy it. The 600 IU of vitamin D3 and 250mg of calcium are
        maintenance amounts that help you reach daily targets, not treatments.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you try a collagen supplement for menopausal aches, give it a fair test: the full dose
        every day for 12 weeks, alongside the strength training, and judge it on whether stairs and
        mornings are easier. If nothing has changed by then, stop.
      </p>

      <h2 id="doctor">When joint pain is not menopause</h2>

      <p>
        Rheumatoid arthritis commonly begins in women between 30 and 60, overlapping the menopause
        transition, which is one reason it gets missed. See a doctor rather than waiting it out if
        you have:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>Swollen, warm or red joints, especially the small joints of the hands or feet.</li>
        <li>Morning stiffness lasting more than about an hour.</li>
        <li>Pain that is worse after rest and better with use, in both hands symmetrically.</li>
        <li>Unexplained weight loss, fever, or a new rash alongside joint pain.</li>
        <li>A single hot, very painful joint, which needs to be seen the same day.</li>
        <li>Bone pain with muscle weakness, such as difficulty rising from a chair, which can point to vitamin D deficiency.</li>
      </ul>

