      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Can menopause cause joint pain?</summary>
          <p>Yes. About 71% of perimenopausal women report musculoskeletal pain, according to a 2024 review in Climacteric that named it the musculoskeletal syndrome of menopause. Falling oestrogen is the most likely common thread, though the mechanism is not fully proven.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Which joints hurt during menopause?</summary>
          <p>Most often the hands, knees, shoulders, hips and feet, frequently with morning stiffness. Many women describe a general ache rather than one painful joint. Swollen or red joints are not typical of menopause and should be checked for inflammatory arthritis.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does HRT help joint pain in menopause?</summary>
          <p>Modestly. In the Women's Health Initiative oestrogen-alone trial, joint pain after one year was reported by 76.3% of women on oestrogen against 79.2% on placebo, with no change in swelling. It is worth discussing with a gynaecologist as a small extra benefit, not as a reason on its own.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does collagen help menopause joint pain?</summary>
          <p>It has not been tested in menopausal women specifically. Collagen trials in knee osteoarthritis show a moderate effect on pain, and a 12-month trial of 12g daily in 302 postmenopausal women is under way. If you try it, take the full dose daily for 12 weeks and stop if nothing changes.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How long does menopause joint pain last?</summary>
          <p>It often eases after the transition, but it is not guaranteed to, because muscle and bone loss continue after menopause. Strength training is the intervention most likely to change the trajectory.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What vitamins help with menopause joint pain?</summary>
          <p>Correcting a measured vitamin D deficiency can help bone pain that comes from the deficiency itself. Beyond that, no vitamin has been shown to relieve menopausal joint pain in a trial. A blood test is more useful than a guess.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>When should I see a doctor about joint pain in my 40s or 50s?</summary>
          <p>If joints are swollen, warm or red, if morning stiffness lasts more than about an hour, or if you have fever or weight loss. Rheumatoid arthritis commonly starts in women in this age range and responds best to early treatment.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

